 "provenance": {
  "chassis": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - 9001-22301 MECHANICAL: 68.11 in (1730 mm) length, 2.05 in (52 mm) width, 2.09 in (53 mm) depth, aluminium chassis, black. The 53 is the body from the face to the back (523.86 to 583.95 pt on the side view); the outlet mouldings stand 3.96 off the face, the breaker plates 7.5, the network module about 9 and its keys 11.0, and the buttons 9.4 off the back, none of it in the 53. The SKU page's PRODUCT HEIGHT 53 mm and WIDTH 52 mm say the same with the axes renamed; the brochure's 68.1 x 2.1 x 2.1 in agrees. Weight 8.3 kg is the SKU page's."
  },
  "colours": {
   "confidence": "photo-measured",
   "note": "photo-measured - the section colours are the modal saturated pixels of the EVMI2130X-OUTLET-BANK_C5, _C6 and _C7 masters, a FIELD on the strip and breaker parts that each placement sets (library/components/README.md, #177). The body is the modal dark grey of the same masters, about #303030; the outlet mouldings are a shade darker in every photograph."
  },
  "dcim": {
   "confidence": "datasheet",
   "note": "the input is one NEMA L21-30P power port and each outlet a power outlet fed by it, `iec-60320-c13` or `eaton-c39` (both types in NetBox and Nautobot, dcim/choices.py). NO feed_leg IS WRITTEN: every outlet is line to line (A on L1-L2, B on L2-L3, C on L3-L1, from the one-line diagram) and a DCIM outlet's feed_leg names one leg. NO `through` IS WRITTEN: it names a bay, and the breakers are fixed parts; each breaker's description names its two sections, and the six section regions name their breaker. THE OUTLETS ARE NAMED AS THE UNIT PRINTS THEM, A1 to C42 (the maintainer's decision, 2026-10-08): an id cannot spell A1, so the `interfaces:` rules map each `outlet-<letter><n>` to its printed label, and the export names the power outlet by it. The labels are read off the EVMI2130X outlet close-ups and the drawing."
  },
  "ends": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - the end view of 9001-22301 is the cord end: a grommet 36.9 across, centred on the width and 28.5 from the back, and the cord 18.2 across 31.1 from the back, beside the button standing off the back. Top and bottom views have y = 0 at the rear. No view draws the far end; it is drawn as its end cap alone."
  },
  "front": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - the front view of 9001-22301, read in drawing mm (x from the cord end, y from the edge drawn at the top). Six outlet mouldings 181.29 long by 36.1 (y 6.0 to 42.1) start at x 195.1, 457.3, 719.6, 1130.7, 1333.7 and 1536.6; each carries seven outlets at a 25.4 pitch (a C13 recess starting 3.24, 54.04, 104.84 and 155.64 into the moulding, a C39 26.54, 77.34 and 128.14) and a printed strip at y 45.5 to 49.5. The breaker plates (60 by 48) start at 131.0, 393.2 and 655.5, the network module's faceplate spans 918.85 to 1106.75 and y 1.45 to 50.6. At the cord end, an M6 ground screw (12.5 across at 52.05, 38.05), a screw 6.0 across at 14.0, 26.0 and a round boss 8.2 across at 92.0, 36.0; a second 6.0 screw at 1723.0, 26.0. End caps 2 thick at both ends."
  },
  "lamps-and-module": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing and datasheet - the network module is eaton/g4-enmc@1, in a bay, because the GNM firmware user guide 2.2 calls it hot-swappable; its jacks, lamps, LCD and keys are read off the same front view and the guide's section 7.1. Its two Ethernet ports are eth0 and eth1, the names the module's LCD shows in EVMI2130X_DT5. The PDU face carries no lamp of its own."
  },
  "left": {
   "confidence": "estimated",
   "note": "estimated - no view draws the other long side. It is drawn as the right side mirrored front to back, which EVMI2130X_SD5 (that side, front to the right) supports in the band it resolves: small fasteners about a third of the depth back at the cord end and the far end. The photograph cannot resolve the hex inserts, so they are carried over from the drawn side."
  },
  "lifecycle": {
   "confidence": "datasheet",
   "note": "searched 2026-10-08 (web, \"Eaton EVMI2130X end of life OR discontinued\") and found nothing; the product is from 2023 (the drawing's first issue). The G4 grid lists an EOL notification document under a /secure/ path, which was not fetched and may not concern this SKU."
  },
  "method": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "FIGURES FROM THE VENDOR'S VECTOR DRAWING. Eaton drawing 9001-22301 (EVMI2130X G4 METERED INPUT, first issue 18-Aug-2023, D size, SCALE 0.400) draws the PDU lying down, cord end at the left, in a front view, a side view below it and a rear view below that, with an end view of the cord end and a one-line diagram. Its views are paths, not a raster, so every position here was read off the PDF's geometry with PyMuPDF at 1.133858 pt per mm (0.4 x 72 / 25.4). The check on that scale is the drawing's own dimensions: 1730 overall lands at 1961.58 pt (1730.0), the 1555.8 between buttons at 1764.06 pt (1555.8), the 72.00 and both 55.80 to within 0.02. The library draws the PDU STANDING, cord end down, as the EVMI2130X_C5 photograph stands it, so a drawing x along the length becomes 1730 minus that, and a drawing y across becomes x. The 3000 px masters staged with the drawing (EVMI2130X_C5, _BK5, _SD5, _DT5 and the outlet, breaker, button and plug close-ups) are the second reading, for colour, the lamps and the printing."
  },
  "mount": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "`rack-side`, which is the library's one way to say a part stands vertically beside the rack's opening, on a post, takes no rack unit and exports u_height 0 and is_full_depth false (docs/vertical-cable-managers-design.md). The G4 installation and setup guide P-164001156 rev 3 says every 0U G4 is \"mounted vertically inside rack structures\" (2.2): its two factory buttons go into the keyhole slots on the rack post (2.11.2), one or two PDUs a side (2.9, Fig. 7), in an enclosure's zero-U channel or on a Tripp Lite two- or four-post rack (2.11). `ru: 39` is the height it runs, the SKU title's 39U; 1730 is 38.9 units. Which side, and front or rear, is a lab placement's to say. The design note that made `rack-side` scoped the in-cabinet zero-U channel out (section 6); this device uses the device-level meaning, which fits, and not that channel."
  },
  "outlet-pitch": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - NOT DIMENSIONED, MEASURED FROM THE PATHS. The C13 noses of section A stand at x 201.7, 252.5, 303.3 and 354.1 (50.8 apart, two outlets) and the C39 recesses at 221.64, 272.44 and 323.24, so the outlet pitch is 25.40, one inch, and the same 50.8 holds in every section. The span checks against something dimensioned: the two buttons are 1555.8 apart on the same sheet at the same scale."
  },
  "rear": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - the rear view of 9001-22301: the two mounting buttons on the width's centre line (the 1.02 [26.00] dimension), 72.00 from the cord end and 1555.8 apart, and a 5.4 threaded hole 55.80 beyond each, at 127.8 and 1683.6, where the buttons can be moved. Both holes lie towards the far end of their buttons, so the second is outboard, not inboard. The view is symmetric about its centre line, so the rear is the view unmirrored."
  },
  "relief": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - the side view of 9001-22301 puts the outlet mouldings 3.96 proud of the face (0.5 at their lip). They are drawn flat: a raised decor box would bury the outlets placed on it in 3D. The outlets are wells, 18 and 20 deep, from their own contracts, and the breaker plates are built 7.5 proud from theirs. The network module is 40 deep (estimated), inside the 53 mm body."
  },
  "sections": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing and photo - 9001-22301 labels the six SECTION A (YELLOW), B (BLUE), C (ORANGE), then the network module, then A, B, C again; the outlet close-ups number them A1-A7, B8-B14, C15-C21, A22-A28, B29-B35 and C36-C42, as the drawing prints them. The one-line diagram puts breaker A across L1 and L2, B across L2 and L3 and C across L3 and L1, each feeding a SECTION box; there are three breakers (CIRCUIT BREAKERS: (3) 20A 2-POLE LISTED) at the head of the first A, B and C, and the second A, B and C have none, so each breaker feeds the two sections of its letter. That reading is the drawing's colour and letter labels; no sentence held says it in words."
  },
  "sides": {
   "confidence": "drawing",
   "note": "drawing - the side view of 9001-22301, the long side at the strip's edge (third angle, below the front view), which is the standing PDU's right; x = 0 is the front. Hex threaded inserts 6.6 across at 72.0, 127.8, 1627.8 and 1683.6, 20.0 behind the face (the 1.30 [33.00] dimension, from the back), are the side-mount positions for the buttons; a Torx screw 6.0 across 20.0 behind the face at 31.9 and 1697.9, and an end-cap screw 5.7 across 32.5 behind the face, 7.0 from each end. A seam runs the length 13.2 to 14.8 behind the face."
  }
 },
